<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/alter_branch.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="sql-alterbranch">
 <indexterm zone="sql-alterbranch">
  <primary>ALTER BRANCH</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>ALTER BRANCH</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>ALTER BRANCH</refname>
  <refpurpose>materialize a shared-readonly database branch as private writable</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
ALTER BRANCH <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> MATERIALIZE WRITABLE
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>ALTER BRANCH ... MATERIALIZE WRITABLE</command> permanently changes
   a branch created with <literal>BUFFER SHARED READ ONLY</literal> into an
   ordinary private-writable branch.  The branch already has cloned storage,
   so materialization does not copy relation files or page bytes.  It drains
   target transactions, invalidates the target database's buffer mappings,
   and lets later reads load pages from the branch's own storage.
  </para>

  <para>
   Materialization uses the persistent
   <link linkend="catalog-pg-dbbranch"><structname>pg_dbbranch</structname></link>
   modes <literal>s</literal>, <literal>m</literal>, and
   <literal>p</literal>.  It first commits <literal>m</literal> as a
   fail-closed state, removes the target's shared-buffer aliases, and only
   then commits <literal>p</literal>.  Existing target transactions drain;
   target transactions that arrive during the operation wait behind the same
   gate.  The wait honors normal lock timeout and cancellation behavior.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of the database branch to materialize.  Run the command while
      connected to another database.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   The command cannot run inside a transaction block.  The caller must own the
   target database or be a superuser, and the target must have a
   <structname>pg_dbbranch</structname> row.
  </para>

  <para>
   If the server stops or the command fails after <literal>m</literal> is
   committed, new target transactions fail closed.  Run the same command from
   another database to repeat buffer cleanup and finish the transition.
   Repeating the command after the branch reaches <literal>p</literal> is a
   successful no-op.
  </para>

  <para>
   This transition is one-way.  It does not make a private branch shared
   again, and it does not change the buffer mode of the source database or
   sibling branches.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   Make <literal>analytics_branch</literal> private and writable:
<programlisting>
ALTER BRANCH analytics_branch MATERIALIZE WRITABLE;
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   There is no <command>ALTER BRANCH</command> statement in the SQL standard.
  </para>
 </refsect1>
</refentry>
